<article class="env-lemma" id="06DD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06DD">Lemma <span data-tag="06DD">10.153.6</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a strictly henselian local ring. Any finite type $R$-algebra $S$ can be written as $S = A_1 \times \ldots \times A_ n \times B$ with $A_ i$ local and finite over $R$ and $\kappa \subset \kappa (\mathfrak m_{A_ i})$ finite purely inseparable and $R \to B$ not quasi-finite at any prime of $B$ lying over $\mathfrak m$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      First write $S = A_1 \times \ldots \times A_ n \times B$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/04GJ" data-tag="04GJ">10.153.5</a>. The field extension $\kappa (\mathfrak m_{A_ i})/\kappa $ is finite and $\kappa $ is separably algebraically closed, hence it is finite purely inseparable. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>